   The balanced equation is:
   \[
   \text{S} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{SO}_2
   \]
   The reaction shows that 1 mole of sulfur reacts with 2 moles of oxygen to produce 1 mole of SO₂. Therefore, the moles of sulfur required are half the moles of oxygen:
   \[
   \text{Moles of sulfur} = \frac{1.94 \text{ mol}}{2} = 0.97 \text{ mol}
   \]

4. **Mass of sulfur required:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of sulfur} = 0.97 \text{ mol} \times 16 \text{ g/mol} = 15.6 \text{ g}
   \]

5. **Mass of SO₂ formed:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of SO₂} = 1.94 \text{ mol} \times 32 \text{ g/mol} = 61.6 \text{ g}
   \]

6. **Mass of residual gases:**
   The remaining oxygen is completely converted to SO₂, so the remaining gases are only the remaining gases in the air (O, N₂, CO₂, etc).
   \[
   \text{Mass of residual gases} = \text{Mass of air} - \text{Mass of sulfur} = 250 \text{ g} - 15.6 \text{ g} = 234.4 \text{ g}
   \]

