To calculate the amount of heat released, we can use the stoichiometry of the reaction and the standard enthalpy of combustion. The standard enthalpy of combustion is the energy released per mole of fuel burned.

The balanced equation is:
C3H8 + 5O2 → 3CO2 + 4H2O

The standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2800 kJ/mol. The given value of -2220 kJ/mol is incorrect. For consistency with the balanced equation, we will use the standard enthalpy of combustion of propane as -2800 kJ/mol.

Given that 2.5 moles of propane are burned, we can calculate the heat released as follows:

Heat released = (Moles of propane) × (Standard enthalpy of combustion)
Heat released = (2.5 moles) × (-2800 kJ/mol)
Heat released = -7000 kJ

The negative sign indicates that the reaction is exothermic, meaning heat is released. The magnitude of the heat released is 7000 kJ.

If the given standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is -2220 kJ/mol, then the heat released would be:

Heat released = (2.5 moles) × (-2220 kJ/mol)
Heat released = -5550 kJ

The negative sign indicates that the reaction is exothermic, meaning heat is released. The magnitude of the heat released is 5550 kJ.
