Consider a p-channel MOSFET with an oxide thickness of 500 Å and a donor doping concentration \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\). Calculate the body-to-source voltage (\(V_{BS}\)) needed to shift the threshold voltage (\(V_T\)) from its value at \(V_{BS} = 0\) by \(-1.0\) V.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The threshold voltage (\(V_T\)) of a p-channel MOSFET is given by:

\[
V_{T} = -V_{bi} - q \phi_i - \frac{q}{2\epsilon_s} \epsilon_s \epsilon_w N_d W_w
\]

where:
- \(q\) is the electron charge (\(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C})\)
- \(\phi_i\) is the built-in potential
- \(\phi_i = \frac{kT}{q} \ln \left( \frac{N_d}{N_a} \right)\)
- \(\epsilon_s\) is the semiconductor permittivity
- \(\epsilon_w\) is the well permittivity
- \(N_d\) is the depletion doping concentration
- \(W_w\) is the width of the oxide region (same as oxide thickness \(W_w = t)\).

The shift in threshold voltage (\(\Delta V_T\)) due to the bias voltage \(V_{BS}\) is given by:

\[
\Delta V_T = \frac{q N_d \phi_i}{2 \varepsilon_s} \left( 1 - \exp\left( -\frac{q V_{BS}}{2 \phi_i} \right) \right)
\]

Given:
- \(\Delta V_T = -1.0 \text{ V}\)
- \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\)
- \(t = 500 \text{ Å} = 500 \times 10^{-10} \text{ cm}\)

First, calculate \(\phi_i\). For a typical silicon material, \(\phi_i \approx 0.025 \text{ V}\).

Next, calculate \(\epsilon_s\). For silicon, \(\epsilon_s \approx 1.1 \times 10^{-14} \text{ F/cm}\).

